{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:43:56.121917Z","id":"d94eb8add4b8da972208a3bda664f33e2aa9f2573e9c1b0f658ebfc00241eda2","body":{"detail":"writes_json clamps `cell` only to 4..1 MiB (board.rs:1438). The H7 has 1060 KiB of RAM (crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs:185-192), so board/writes/0/4 yields up to 271,360 cells of `ram,n,stamp` (up to ~20 bytes each) in one string, and each cell calls `bus.rams.iter().position(..)` (board.rs:1450). writes_in_json can do the same for a whole 512 KiB RAM. Every other memory read caps itself at MEMORY_SERVED = 4096 (board.rs:74, used by written_json at board.rs:1400); these two routes do not, so a page bug or a hand-typed path makes the kernel allocate megabytes inside the container's linear memory, which is then carried into checkpoints.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1437},"end":{"line":1459},"content_hash":"444a106f7d935fd752ed078c5b760c6dde5d1ae575fbf9bdbc5e51e3d4ee1240"},"suggested_fix":"Bound the answer: raise the minimum cell for writes_json so that total RAM / cell stays under a fixed count (e.g. 65,536 cells), and clamp writes_in_json's length / cell the same way. Have written_since return the RAM's index instead of its base so writes_json need not search for it per cell.","summary":"board/writes has no size bound: at cell 4 with since 0 an H7 answer is about 271k cells (~5 MB of JSON), built with an O(cells x RAMs) lookup","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:47:23.193221Z","id":"6a7028eac26bb2bc791a9157a572175f2197c2c6cdbce9e8617cce08b80ff012","body":{"detail":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs:1438 clamps cell only to 4..1 MiB; the H7's RAMs at crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs:185-192 total 512+128+288+64+4+64 = 1060 KiB = 271,360 4-byte cells; board.rs:1450 runs `bus.rams.iter().position(..)` per cell; writes_in_json (board.rs:1465-1469) has no length cap either, while written_json caps at MEMORY_SERVED = 4096 (board.rs:74, 1401).","kind":"comment","references":"d94eb8add4b8da972208a3bda664f33e2aa9f2573e9c1b0f658ebfc00241eda2","summary":"confirmed: board/writes is unbounded, ~271k cells for the H7 at cell 4, with a per-cell RAM search","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504363Z","id":"ba28716150b8011a4b7f539818e90014a4861fb25c1321f3bb834a1e7485fb10","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:5f3e6d9","summary":"fixed: crates/kernel/src/board.rs cell_for makes cells large enough that an answer has at most CELLS_SERVED = 65536, for writes_json over all RAMs and writes_in_json over the clamped range, and the answer reports the cell used; written_since now returns the RAM index, so no per-cell search. The page checks the cell answered (web/memory.js poll, detail.cell === zoom.cell).","supersedes":"d94eb8add4b8da972208a3bda664f33e2aa9f2573e9c1b0f658ebfc00241eda2","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:03.272827Z","id":"ef948d06e43444c264ccd0bb5186329aa53db839b51748b6f4a8adac87af38b8","body":{"detail":"machine.receive refuses bytes while the receiver is off or its line queue is full; the key is then lost without a word. A complete version would queue them, or answer how many were taken.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":619},"end":{"line":632},"content_hash":"8fc413ce106221dde0177bcf9b15754c6eea3150887c0470d66081b375c54a6b"},"summary":"keys drops bytes the USART cannot take, silently","tags":["session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:03.272881Z","id":"b0e05947b610ad88998cb1461d802968ecb97c7c01e479be7de4836cb6a826b9","body":{"detail":"Fine at 35 frames a second in Chrome and Node, but a binary read, or a diff against the frame asked with, would cut the worker-to-page traffic several-fold.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1134},"end":{"line":1154},"content_hash":"d5d318528ba706bbb1bdf630e7d45abc3501d42f927f39c149df69fdbd2ac931"},"summary":"board/display sends each new frame whole, as base64 JSON, about 85 KiB","tags":["session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:34.327677Z","id":"da4a5817c7021cdc426259e37af60793a1e8ced8d34dc0096ff2bf209469acfd","body":{"detail":"Commit f4de074 changed `pointer` (board.rs:1580, 1894, and the inline copy at 1974) from `ram_at(value).is_some() || value >= 0x0800_0000` to `value >= 0x0800_0000 && self.machine.bus.is_memory(value)`. `Bus::is_memory` (crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs:365) is true only for flash, the RAMs, the QUADSPI window and system memory, not for peripherals (0x4000_0000 and up, or the PPB at 0xE000_0000). Hubris drivers keep peripheral pointers in structs: drv/stm32xx-i2c/src/lib.rs:69 has `pub registers: &'a RegisterBlock`. When following everything from partway through a run, that struct was written before the window, so a status read through it, such as `ldr r0, [r1, #0x18]`, is a Load whose address comes from a `Held { size: 4, value: 0x4000_5c00 }`. `says_nothing` now returns false for that origin. `Provenance::through` then keeps the Load, and the tree gains a `chose` operand that only names which peripheral. `influences_json` lists the base as an influence, and `why_steps` lists it as an address leaf. Before this commit all three left it out, because its value was >= 0x0800_0000. The negative-number case the commit set out to fix concerned small negative integers, not addresses inside the peripheral regions.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1892},"end":{"line":1902},"content_hash":"eb7d49cea4566a29e05d1ebde79f61c3380a4e33ceb8b1ab7537997171b11681"},"suggested_fix":"Add an address test on the bus that is true for memory or a mapped peripheral (Target::Memory or any peripheral target in Bus::decode), and use it as the pointer test, so that 0xFFFF_FFF0 is still excluded and 0x4000_5C00 is included.","summary":"The pointer test now treats peripheral base addresses as data, so a register block pointer held in RAM shows up as what chose a lookup","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:34.327800Z","id":"31b4f64aa1e1286c324c41997fb875a0972e662b8d83cbeecdf9543be0ae8545","body":{"detail":"Three places in board.rs decide which origins only name a table: `says_nothing` in derive_json (1895-1902), the leaf filter in influences_json (1595-1600), and the Load-leaf filters in why_steps (1972-1974). Each has its own copy of the pointer test, and the three already disagree. derive_json treats a 4-byte `Store` of a pointer constant (a Store with parents[0] == 0, which `origins` does return) as saying nothing. why_steps does not, so board/why still lists that store under a Load's `address`, while board/derive leaves it out. The `What::Load { value, .. } | What::Bytes { value, .. }` arms at 1901 are unreachable: `says_nothing` is only applied to what `Provenance::origins` returns (provenance.rs:414-434), and `origins` walks through Load to parents[0] and through Bytes to its lanes. It never returns either kind. The comment on those arms ('or loaded: which struct, which table') describes a rule that the code does not apply. The `moves` closure is also copied, at 1583 and 1623.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1892},"end":{"line":1902},"content_hash":"eb7d49cea4566a29e05d1ebde79f61c3380a4e33ceb8b1ab7537997171b11681"},"suggested_fix":"Make the pointer test and the says-nothing predicate one method of Board, for example `fn says_nothing(&self, node: &Node) -> bool`. Use it in derive_json, influences_json and why_steps, remove the unreachable Load and Bytes arms or change origins so that they can match, and move `moves` into a method too.","summary":"The rule for which origins say nothing exists in three copies that already disagree, and two arms of says_nothing can never match","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:34.327881Z","id":"e6fed0d6a6fbd387c78e5c4de202ba24b750300bd2bb1aba478bac758388c967","body":{"detail":"When `ops::immediate` finds no immediate, node_json falls back to `What::Merge::constant`, which `Provenance::settle` (provenance.rs:308) sets to the value of the lowest-numbered register the instruction read that had no tag. It emits that value as `\"imm\"`, the same key as a constant decoded from the instruction. `web/derive.js` (line 340 onward) then calls it 'a constant the code had made before'. A register without a tag is not necessarily a constant. With `board/provenance on`, only bus reads are tagged, so in `adds r0, r0, r3` with r3 a loop index, the index's value at that moment is served as `imm`. Following everything, a counter that only immediates ever touched has no tag either. The tree then shows `x + 0x5` as if the 5 were fixed, but the next iteration adds 6. When the instruction reads two registers without tags, only the lower-numbered one is reported.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1857},"end":{"line":1863},"content_hash":"9d248dae4477c65fc0ebb83f217a0acde1dfa7bafed92195b82f68552f7c9d3c"},"suggested_fix":"Emit the fallback under its own key, for example `\"held\":{\"register\":\"r3\",\"value\":5}` (keep the register number in What::Merge), and keep `imm` for constants decoded from the instruction. Have derive.js say 'what r3 held' rather than 'a constant' unless the provenance is following everything.","summary":"node_json gives an untagged register's run-time value the same `imm` key as an immediate encoded in the instruction, so consumers cannot tell them apart","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:34.327951Z","id":"52a10fe4bb68e30c7248282cb826805a25efb9f9511b2fd6fe5b2adb1f1fe277","body":{"detail":"Provenance::derive (provenance.rs:546-551) follows `copied_from` with no limit and does not take anything from `walk.budget`. json() then calls node_json on each copy, and each call does a DWARF `lines_at`, `code_at` and `describe_typed`. A value stored again unchanged on every loop iteration produces one copy per iteration. Examples are a running max that stays the same (`cmp; it; mov` keeps the tag) or `x |= 0` (settle keeps the tag when the value does not change). After a window of N iterations, a board/derive answer contains N copy steps, and the 400-node budget passed at board.rs:1904 does not limit it. The page (web/derive.js:153, 294) goes through all of them as well.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1913},"end":{"line":1916},"content_hash":"f857a73196c2b2e41d058f1500ed327404e3fcb30e5b9910a984a7b3d259f21c"},"suggested_fix":"Limit the copies collected in Provenance::derive (for example to 16), keep the total count in Derived (for example `copies_total`), and emit `\"copies_more\":N` so the page can say how many were left out. Alternatively, take each copy from walk.budget.","summary":"derive_json renders every copy in a store's copy chain in full, with no limit and outside the 400-node budget","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:34.328010Z","id":"3177b071e045f4cbfd852ccb6d4114df85c2b6eae1a4a1f5d34bd824963e58e4","body":{"detail":"Before this range, why_steps rendered a Load with `describe_typed(byte, ...)`. `byte` is what `Provenance::story_from` passes for a Load, `addr + lane` (provenance.rs:403). The new shared node_json renders `What::Load` with `describe_typed(addr, ...)` and ignores `byte`, although Store and Held go through `within(addr, size)`. When the followed byte is lane 1-3 of a word load from a computed address, for example a u8 field at offset 2 of a struct read with an indexed `ldr`, `where` now names the field at offset 0.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":1866},"end":{"line":1866},"content_hash":"f548c24dfa2c5a2d214860b60f05e3636db9ee86bc606f908a313da741ce3b3a"},"suggested_fix":"Describe the byte for a Load too: `quoted(&self.describe_typed(within(addr, 4), map, read))`. Better, keep the load's size in `What::Load` and pass that size to `within`.","summary":"board/why now gives a Load step the location of the load's base address, not of the byte being followed","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:34.328064Z","id":"553c4c1c48dfbe9e635aee02d07dadc487f68707593a8892e3d616e75cb95e57","body":{"detail":"For `What::Bytes`, Provenance::derive (provenance.rs:580-585) pushes each lane's tag as a `Role::Value` operand, removing duplicates. json() renders the operands with `byte` 0 and no lane index. A word put together from bytes that came from different places, such as `u32::from_le_bytes` over four I2C reads, shows up in board/derive as a list of operands with no positions. When two lanes share a tag, the removal of duplicates shifts the order, so the position cannot be inferred either. board/why follows only the lane of the byte asked about (story_from, provenance.rs:392-396), so the two routes describe the same byte differently.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":1905},"end":{"line":1931},"content_hash":"ee02498936e5b72298e02c085203f9bd0dad730a799c830d67a1edc04dc8ad20"},"suggested_fix":"Keep the lane numbers for each operand of a Bytes node, for example `operands: Vec<(Role, Derived)>` with a `lanes: u8` bitmask on Derived. Emit `\"lanes\":[0,2]` in json(), and pass the matching byte address rather than 0.","summary":"A Bytes node's operands in the derive tree carry no lane, so the reader cannot tell which part made the byte asked about","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/server.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:34.328115Z","id":"bd075f260a492f0d59e1bd02a6899b0aef2f9f1c5c7bcbc3215e6f4c7a8f0bb1","body":{"detail":"Commit 19c8ecc keeps lr's value as a merge's constant, and the Register origin for r14 is rendered as \"lr\" (board.rs:1839-1847). The route parses its subject with Subject::parse (board.rs:87-91), which accepts only r0-r12. web/derive.js `onwards` (line 134) likewise follows only `^r\\d+$`. When following everything, a value that reaches back to lr's contents at the start of the window ends there, with no request that can continue it into the window before. r0-r12 can be continued that way.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":243},"end":{"line":247},"content_hash":"a53ac62ae204aee5ad83341a3823545e27e9310bb702f3380889d64a667c4e30"},"suggested_fix":"Let Subject::parse accept r14 or `lr` (n < 15 except 13), and let derive.js's `onwards` continue a `lr` register step with `board/derive/r14`.","summary":"board/derive cannot follow lr back, though merges now treat lr's value as a constant source","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:34.328175Z","id":"aec2e63ce5d6bac288721473aab23ff7c12d0f7256fbf21b9e8f663837349f1f","body":{"detail":"No test in crates/kernel (src or tests/) calls derive_json, node_json, influences_json or the `board/derive` path. A search for `derive_json|node_json|board/derive` matches only board.rs and server.rs. The JSON is built by hand with format! and string slicing (`&step[1..]`, `&why[..why.len() - 1]`), and the page depends on its keys (`op`, `imm`, `bits`, `rshift`, `shift`, `role`, `cut`, `same`, `pointer`, `repeats`, `earliest`). Nothing checks that the output parses or that `n<tag>` routes to a node rather than an address.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":1815},"end":{"line":1940},"content_hash":"063808106876dfeab9e901e32636dc7f04022ab428294bf4044755575f7dad1e"},"suggested_fix":"Add a kernel test that runs a few instructions under `Provenance::everything` (for example a literal-pool load, a register-indexed `ldr`, `add.w r4, r4, r0, lsr #26` and a counter loop), calls derive_json for an address, a register and `n<tag>`, parses the result with serde_json, and checks the keys the page reads.","summary":"node_json's merge decoding, derive_json and the board/derive route have no tests","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:06:01.199197Z","id":"d2853624d264f9e75f65eedfb4280c94b63b22e6148fa998b1b134f97498e557","body":{"detail":"`git show f4de074 -- crates/kernel/src/board.rs` replaces `ram_at(value).is_some() || value >= 0x0800_0000` with `value >= 0x0800_0000 && self.machine.bus.is_memory(value)` at board.rs:1580, 1894 and the inline filter at 1973. crates/armv6m/src/bus.rs:365-399: is_memory matches only Target::Memory (flash, RAMs, QUADSPI window, flash alias, system memory); peripheral addresses decode to Target::Soc or Target::Scs, so is_memory(0x4000_5c00) is false. A Held{size:4,value:0x4000_5c00} now fails says_nothing (board.rs:1896), passes the influences leaf filter (1595), and passes the why_steps filter (1973), whereas the old `>= 0x0800_0000` rule excluded it. /Users/alex/Devel/misc/hubris/drv/stm32xx-i2c/src/lib.rs:69 `pub registers: &'a RegisterBlock` shows such pointers live in structs.","kind":"comment","references":"da4a5817c7021cdc426259e37af60793a1e8ced8d34dc0096ff2bf209469acfd","summary":"confirmed: peripheral base pointers no longer count as pointers after f4de074","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:06:01.199237Z","id":"0ae48344bf1ac9e3b11b1cab241818159328aa3e537852ceed441b99cee75d7d","body":{"detail":"board.rs:1901 says_nothing treats `What::Store { size: 4, value }` with pointer(value) as saying nothing; why_steps filters at 1970-1973 only exclude Held near pc and Held{size:4}/Register pointers, not Store. provenance.rs:430 pushes a Store to origins' output when parents[0]==0 (the `_ => out.push` arm at 432), so such a Store reaches both. provenance.rs:427 and 429 always walk through Bytes and Load, never output them, and only_pointers (provenance.rs ~609-612) applies says_nothing solely to origins() results, so the Load/Bytes arms at board.rs:1901 cannot match. The `moves` closure appears at board.rs:1583 (influences_json) and 1623 (makers_json, memoized variant).","kind":"comment","references":"31b4f64aa1e1286c324c41997fb875a0972e662b8d83cbeecdf9543be0ae8545","summary":"confirmed: three diverging copies of the says-nothing rule; Load/Bytes arms unreachable","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:06:01.199250Z","id":"b907ee83bd90f001afa11f9dbd92b9b39772b4f740ebbbdb32229752a28b6a93","body":{"detail":"provenance.rs:308 `constant = (0..15).find(|&n| n != 13 && read & 1 << n != 0 && self.regs[n] == 0).map(|n| self.values[n])` takes the lowest-numbered untagged register read and its run-time value (self.values updated at provenance.rs:356). board.rs:1857-1862 emits it as `\"imm\":{v}` in the `None` fallback, identical to `Immediate::Value`. web/derive.js:340 and 358-361 describe step.imm as 'a constant the code had made before' and use it as the fixed other operand. Nothing in the tag check establishes the register is constant across executions.","kind":"comment","references":"e6fed0d6a6fbd387c78e5c4de202ba24b750300bd2bb1aba478bac758388c967","summary":"confirmed: an untagged register's current value is emitted under the same `imm` key as a decoded immediate","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:06:01.199262Z","id":"6a6f8b1289112e56428ff380f8353c12114378b823df7887a8e23852b30f8657","body":{"detail":"provenance.rs:546-551 `while let Some(before) = self.copied_from(at, walk) { out.copies.push(before); at = before; }` has no count limit and does not decrement walk.budget (decremented once per derive at provenance.rs:545). copied_from (provenance.rs:637-644) passes through a Load whose address says nothing (through, provenance.rs:509-530) back to the earlier Store, so each same-value re-store of a static links to the previous one. board.rs:1913-1916 renders every copy with node_json (lines_at, code_at, describe_typed). web/derive.js:153 and 294 iterate `[step, ...copies]`.","kind":"comment","references":"52a10fe4bb68e30c7248282cb826805a25efb9f9511b2fd6fe5b2adb1f1fe277","summary":"confirmed: the copy chain is unbounded and outside the node budget","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:06:01.199272Z","id":"bc522ac25a49f7afeac8cf5c49437d70c9b79387fa34ac6625a4ec004c39f56f","body":{"detail":"`git show 713b6ae^:crates/kernel/src/board.rs` lines 1866-1884: why_steps rendered a Load with `quoted(&self.describe_typed(byte, map, read))`. Current board.rs:1866 renders `What::Load { addr, value }` with `describe_typed(addr, ...)`, while Store and Held at 1826/1837 use `within(addr, size)`. provenance.rs:403 passes `addr.wrapping_add(lane)` as the byte for a Load, and lane is set nonzero by a word Store at provenance.rs:384, so for lanes 1-3 the described location regressed to offset 0.","kind":"comment","references":"3177b071e045f4cbfd852ccb6d4114df85c2b6eae1a4a1f5d34bd824963e58e4","summary":"confirmed: Load's `where` now describes addr, not the followed byte","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:06:01.199280Z","id":"2a826d6601e952e511b64cf76f49ffa313ff5802507ede1c46a10577968211b5","body":{"detail":"provenance.rs:580-585 pushes each nonzero lane once (`!operands.iter().any(|&(_, t)| t == lane)`) as Role::Value with no lane index; board.rs:1931 renders every operand with byte 0 (`json(board, p, o, *role, 0, map, read)`), and Derived carries no lane field. story_from (provenance.rs:387-391) follows only `lanes[lane]` for board/why. Kind suggestion is appropriate.","kind":"comment","references":"553c4c1c48dfbe9e635aee02d07dadc487f68707593a8892e3d616e75cb95e57","summary":"confirmed: Bytes operands lose their lane positions in board/derive","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/kernel/src/server.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:06:01.199294Z","id":"95c9fa5fd257b438e1cc8fc0d4e01e72d8957c84e9e7586654526b2d47036878","body":{"detail":"board.rs:89 `n.parse::<u8>().ok().filter(|&n| n < 13)` rejects r14 (and 'lr' parses as an address via number()); server.rs:243-247 uses Subject::parse for board/derive. board.rs:1843 renders Register n=14 as \"lr\"; web/derive.js:134 `if (!/^r\\d+$/.test(step.register ?? \"\")) return null;` stops there. provenance.rs:305-308 includes lr among registers whose value becomes a merge's constant. Kind suggestion is appropriate.","kind":"comment","references":"bd075f260a492f0d59e1bd02a6899b0aef2f9f1c5c7bcbc3215e6f4c7a8f0bb1","summary":"confirmed: neither the route nor the page can continue an lr origin","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
